<# One job, N shards inside it -- not `parallel: N`, which is N jobs on N
   runners. Each of those paid for a container start, a pip install and a
   download of the build artifacts to run one Nth of the suite; for a suite
   measured in minutes that overhead was most of the wall clock, and it scaled
   with the shard count instead of against it.

   xmsconan_test_shards is what makes one container viable: N runners writing
   to one log interleave line by line, so it buffers each shard's output and
   replays it whole, then merges the per-shard gtest XML into the single
   TEST-cxxtest.xml reported below. It also does the artifact spelunking that
   used to be inline shell here -- restoring the runner's executable bit and
   linking test_files/ back to the absolute path the binary was compiled with.

   One job per *uninstrumented* testing configuration, each naming its own
   directory with --label and consuming its own build job's artifacts. An
   instrumented testing configuration gets no job here: it runs its suite
   inside its build, because gcovr has to read the .gcda it produces against
   the .gcno written beside them at compile time, and splitting the run out
   would mean shipping a 245 MiB build folder to reach 5.5 MiB of data.

   The labels come from the same config_label() the build names its
   directories with, so the two cannot drift. #>
